            <td>1.784e-01</td>
            <td>1.784e-01</td>
            <td>5.70e-06</td>
            <td>1.784e-01</td>
            <td>4.84e-12</td>
            <td>1.784e-01</td>
            <td>6.02e-14</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </table-wrap>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Verification of all three algorithms against
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[f(t) = 1/\sqrt{\pi t}]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:msqrt><mml:mrow><mml:mi>π</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msqrt></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
      Top: numerical inversions overlaid on the analytical solution.
      Bottom: relative error on a log scale.
      <styled-content id="figU003Averification"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="verification_func1.png" />
    </fig>
    <p><xref alt="[fig:timing]" rid="figU003Atiming">[fig:timing]</xref>
    shows timing per inversion call as a function of the algorithm
    parameter (N, n, or M) for <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[F(s) = 1/(s+1)]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>F</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>s</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>s</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    at <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[t = 1]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
    Solid lines are C++, dashed lines are Python. The Python bindings
    add minimal overhead since the inversion itself runs in compiled
    C++. Stehfest is the cheapest at low parameter counts, De Hoog
    becomes the most expensive at high orders. Talbot sits in
    between.</p>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Time per inversion (<inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\mu]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>μ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>s)
      vs algorithm parameter for C++ (solid) and Python (dashed). The
      Python overhead is small because the inversion runs in compiled
      code.
      <styled-content id="figU003Atiming"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="benchmark_timing.png" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
  <sec id="selected-example-groundwater-well-dipole">
    <title>Selected example: groundwater well dipole</title>
    <p>The well dipole example
    (<xref alt="[fig:dipole_time]" rid="figU003Adipole_time">[fig:dipole_time]</xref>,
    <xref alt="[fig:dipole_spatial]" rid="figU003Adipole_spatial">[fig:dipole_spatial]</xref>)
    shows NILT applied to a 2-D groundwater problem with no radial
    symmetry. A pumping well (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[Q_p = 0.010]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mi>p</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.010</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    m<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[^3]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi></mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>/s)
    and an injection well (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[Q_i = 0.007]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>Q</mml:mi><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.007</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    m<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[^3]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi></mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>/s)
    are placed at different locations in a confined aquifer
    (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[T = 10^{-3}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    m<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[^2]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi></mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>/s,
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[S = 10^{-4}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>S</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>4</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>).
    The Laplace-domain solution is a superposition of
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[K_0]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>K</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    Bessel functions, inverted pointwise at each
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[(x, y)]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>y</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    grid node.</p>
    <p><xref alt="[fig:dipole_time]" rid="figU003Adipole_time">[fig:dipole_time]</xref>
    compares the numerical inversions against the analytical
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[E_1]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    solution over time at a fixed observation point. All three methods
    track the analytical curve, Stehfest shows larger relative error at
    early times (around <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[10^{-1}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
    while Talbot and De Hoog stay below <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[10^{-10}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>10</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
    <p><xref alt="[fig:dipole_spatial]" rid="figU003Adipole_spatial">[fig:dipole_spatial]</xref>
    shows the net drawdown field at <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[t = 2]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    h. The pumping well (bottom-left) draws water down, the injection
    well (top-right) pushes it up, and the asymmetric contours reflect
    their different rates. This is the kind of spatial field that
    requires thousands of pointwise inversions, when a compiled backend
    matters.</p>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Net drawdown vs time for the well dipole. Top: all
      three algorithms overlaid on the analytical solution. Bottom:
      relative error.
      <styled-content id="figU003Adipole_time"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="groundwater_well_dipole.png" />
    </fig>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Spatial drawdown field at
      <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[t = 2]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      h for the pumping-injection dipole. The pumping well
      (<inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\blacktriangledown]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>▾</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
      and injection well (<inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\blacktriangle]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>▴</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
      produce an asymmetric drawdown pattern.
      <styled-content id="figU003Adipole_spatial"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="groundwater_well_dipole_spatial.png" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact">
  <title>Research impact</title>
  <p>NILT was developed during a PhD on reactive transport modelling
  using continuous time random walks
  (<xref alt="Rodolfo Oliveira, 2021" rid="ref-oliveira2021" ref-type="bibr">Rodolfo
  Oliveira, 2021</xref>) and the derived works R. Oliveira et al.
  (<xref alt="2023" rid="ref-oliveira2023" ref-type="bibr">2023</xref>),
  where the Laplace inversion routines were used to compute
  first-passage time distributions and breakthrough curves in
  heterogeneous porous media. The library has since been extracted and
  released as standalone software.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>AI tools were used for code refactoring, and documentation editing.
  All scientific content, algorithm implementations, and mathematical
  formulations are the author’s own work.</p>
</sec>
</body>
